import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/GuardLimits
import ../../../Demos/GuardPlayground
import ../../../Demos/GuardClients
import ../../../Demos/GuardFlags
import ../../../Demos/GuardCheckout
import ../../../Demos/GuardHeaders
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Ui/Demo

## The guard package's page: rate limits, feature flags and small request guards, as middleware.
## Its tables are the package's own answers, worked out when the site is built; one demo runs a
## rate limit in the browser, with a clock moved by hand.
meta = {
	title: "Guard",
	description: "Rate limits, feature flags and request guards for Roc Solid apps, as middleware, decided by plain Roc that a test runs with a fake clock.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "first", label: "A first guard" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Rate limits" },
		{ id: "try", label: "Try a limit" },
		{ id: "proxies", label: "Behind a proxy" },
		{ id: "flags", label: "Feature flags" },
		{ id: "guards", label: "Other guards" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Tested, and limits" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/guard</code>]}
				title="Rate limits, feature flags and guards, as middleware"
			>
				Guard protects an app's routes: 5 login tries a minute from each address, a new checkout for staff
				first and then a quarter of everyone, security headers on every answer, and maintenance mode for
				the whole site. Each guard is <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a> that decides in
				plain Roc, so the tables on this page are the package's own answers, worked out when the site was
				built.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in your app's header (<code>main.roc</code>):
				<code>{"guard: \"../packages/guard/main.roc\""}</code>. Its middleware needs the server platform; the
				browser's half sees only the modules that need no platform (the logic, and the
				<code>{"<Flagged>"}</code> component).
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>In the server's modules (your middleware, your endpoints): <code>import guard.Guard</code>, and <code>guard.FlagStore</code> for flags.</li>
				<li>In a <code>.rocx</code> file: <code>import guard.Flagged</code>, which <code>rocx build</code> copies into the app.</li>
				<li>
					Optional: to keep feature flags' overrides across a restart, an app with a database lists the
					package's <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a>,
					<code>{"plugins: [FlagStore.plugin({})]"}</code> (<a href="#flags">feature flags</a>).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="first">A first guard</Heading>
			<p>
				A guard goes in the <a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a> of the directory it
				guards: <code>routes/+middleware.roc</code> runs around every request, a directory's
				<code>+middleware.roc</code> around the routes in and below it. Here every answer gets security
				headers, a body over a megabyte is refused, the site can go into maintenance, <code>/api</code> is
				limited per user, and the login route per address:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_middlewares.file} source={Sources.guard_middlewares.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_api_middleware.file} source={Sources.guard_api_middleware.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_route.file} source={Sources.guard_route.rocx} />
			<p>
				The sixth try in a minute gets <code>429 Too Many Requests</code>, with <code>Retry-After</code> and
				the <code>RateLimit-*</code> headers. Every guard hands on its context unchanged, so guards go
				anywhere in a chain. The code is from <code>packages/guard/example/</code>, a whole app its tests
				ask over HTTP.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Rate limits</Heading>
			<p>
				A limit says how many requests a client may send, in how long. <code>RateLimit.per_minute(5)</code>
				is a token bucket: 5 at once, then one every 12 seconds. <code>.sliding()</code> counts a sliding
				window instead: never more than 5 in any minute. Both are plain functions: a client's state and
				the time go in; whether the request may go on, and the client's next state, come out.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_limits.file} source={Sources.guard_limits.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The same eleven requests under each limit, as <code>RateLimit.check</code> answered them when this
				page was built (the clock is a list of seconds, as in the package's tests):
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardLimits />
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				In the bucket, a refused request costs nothing, so hammering doesn't delay the next allowed one. The
				sliding window refuses
				everything after the fifth until the five weigh less: its count is this minute's requests plus last
				minute's, weighted by how much of last minute the window still covers. So it keeps two numbers per
				client, however high the limit.
			</p>

			<Heading id="try">Try a limit</Heading>
			<p>
				The same <code>RateLimit.check</code>, in your browser, with a clock you move by hand. Nothing is
				sent: each line is what the middleware would answer.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo>
			<div data-demo="guard-limit"><GuardPlayground /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<details>
				<summary>The demo: <code>{Sources.guard_playground.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.guard_playground.file} source={Sources.guard_playground.rocx} />
			</details>

			<h3>Who is counted</h3>
			<p>
				<code>Guard.rate_limit(name, limit, key)</code> counts each key separately. The
				<code>name</code> keeps one limit's counts apart from another's (<code>"login"</code>,
				<code>"api"</code>). The key says who a request counts as:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Counts</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.ip(trust)</code></td><td>each address, found as <a href="#proxies">trust</a> says; an IPv6 address by its first 64 bits, the block one household is given. Clients whose address can't be told (an <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> that isn't an address, where it is trusted) all count as one, <code>unknown</code>, so a made-up header doesn't make a client unlimited</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.user(|context| context.user, trust)"}</code></td><td>each signed-in user, as the context names them; each address when signed out</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.route</code></td><td>each method and path</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.both(key, key)</code></td><td>each pair: <code>Guard.both(Guard.ip(trust), Guard.route)</code> is each address on each route</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"|request, context| …"}</code></td><td>whatever your function says. A request whose key is <code>""</code> isn't limited</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<h3>What a client is told</h3>
			<p>
				An allowed answer carries <code>RateLimit-Limit</code>, <code>RateLimit-Remaining</code>,
				<code>RateLimit-Reset</code> (seconds until the whole limit is back) and
				<code>RateLimit-Policy</code> (<code>5;w=60</code>: 5 in 60 seconds). A refusal is a <code>429</code>
				with those headers, <code>Retry-After</code> (seconds until one more is allowed) and a line of text.
				A form posted without the runtime goes back to its page with what was typed and a flash of the
				<code>429</code>, shown where the form shows any refusal.
			</p>

			<h3>Memory stays bounded</h3>
			<p>
				Each limit keeps at most 10,000 clients (<code>Guard.rate_limit_with</code> takes another number).
				Clients whose limit is whole again are forgotten first, which changes nothing; past that, the client
				seen longest ago is forgotten and starts again. Each limit's clients are split into 64 tallies, so a
				request reads and writes about 150 of them at most.
			</p>
			<p>
				The counts live in the server's memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>), never in the database.
				A request reads its tally and swaps the next one in, reading again if another request changed it
				first, so no hit is lost. If a tally can't be changed after many tries, the request goes on and the
				log says why: a failing limiter shouldn't take the site down. A restart resets every limit.
			</p>

			<Heading id="proxies">Behind a proxy</Heading>
			<p>
				Most apps sit behind a proxy, which adds the address it saw to <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>. A
				client can write anything in that header too, so it is read only as far as the app says its own
				proxies wrote it. Take a request from 10.0.0.2, the app's proxy, whose
				<code>X-Forwarded-For</code> is <code>6.6.6.6, 198.51.100.4</code>: the client wrote the first
				address, the proxy added the second. Who the client is, by each way to trust, as
				<code>ClientIp.of</code> worked it out when this page was built:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardClients />
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li><code>Direct</code> takes the connection's address and ignores the headers: for when nothing is in front of the server.</li>
				<li><code>Hops(1)</code> says one proxy is in front, and finds the address it saw. <code>Hops(2)</code> claims two, so here it trusts what the client wrote.</li>
				<li>
					<code>Proxies</code> walks the list from the right, past the app's own proxies
					(<code>Ip.private</code>: the loopback and private ranges, or <code>{"Ip.cidrs([\"10.0.0.0/8\"])"}</code>).
					It checks the connection came from one of them, so a client reaching the server directly can't
					name anyone it likes.
				</li>
				<li><code>Header(name)</code> reads one header a proxy sets, such as <code>fly-client-ip</code> or <code>cf-connecting-ip</code>.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<code>Proxies(Ip.private)</code> is right both with no proxy in front and behind one in the server's
				own network, so the app <code>rocx new</code> makes uses it. <code>Hops(1)</code> with no proxy in
				front believes the client, who could name a new address each time and never be limited.
			</p>

			<Heading id="flags">Feature flags</Heading>
			<p>
				A flag is defined in code: its name, what it's for, and who has it on. A rule is on or off for
				everyone (off is a kill switch), plus the users and groups it lists, plus a share of everyone else
				signed in. A hash of the flag's name and the user's puts each user in or out of a rollout the same
				way every time, so raising the share only adds people. An environment can have its own rule.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_flag.file} source={Sources.guard_flag.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Who has it on, as <code>Flags.is_on</code> answered when this page was built. A user's bucket, 0 to
				99, is that hash (<code>Flags.bucket</code>): a rollout of 25 takes those below 25.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<GuardFlags />
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				ada is listed and bea is in staff, so they have it whatever their bucket. A signed-out visitor has
				no id to hash, so a rollout reaches them only at 100.
 The environment is <code>APP_ENV</code> if set, else <code>dev</code> under
				<code>--dev</code>, else <code>production</code>.
			</p>

			<h3>Changed while the server runs</h3>
			<p>
				The code's rule is the default; an admin page can override it until it is reset. Overrides live in
				the server's memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>), so a restart brings back the code's rules.
				To keep them across restarts and deploys, an app with a database lists
				<code>{"FlagStore.plugin({})"}</code>: each override is also a row of its table
				(<code>guard_flags</code>, for Postgres, SQLite and Turso), read back as the server starts. Only
				admins may set a rule: in the example, <code>Middlewares.admins</code> answers anyone else
				<code>403</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_overrides.file} source={Sources.guard_overrides.rocx} />
			<p>
				On the server, <code>{"FlagStore.is_on!(flag, { user, groups })"}</code> answers in an endpoint,
				and <code>Guard.feature(flag, who)</code> answers 404 to anyone without the flag, as if the route
				weren't there.
			</p>

			<h3>In a page</h3>
			<p>
				The server decides the visitor's flags once, in a GET endpoint the layout reads; a
				<code>{"<Flagged>"}</code> shows its children only when its flag is on. The flags travel in the
				rendered page, so the browser hydrates with the same values without asking again.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_visitor.file} source={Sources.guard_visitor.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_layout.file} source={Sources.guard_layout.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_page.file} source={Sources.guard_page.rocx} />
			<p>
				This site has no server, so below the flags are decided as the site is built. ada is listed for the
				new checkout; cy isn't, nor in the rollout's quarter:
			</p>
			<div class="guard-checkouts">
				<GuardCheckout who={{ user: "ada", groups: [] }} />
				<GuardCheckout who={{ user: "cy", groups: [] }} />
			</div>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_checkout.file} source={Sources.guard_checkout.rocx} />
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>{"<Flagged>"} prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>name : Str</code></td><td>the flag's name</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>fallback : Html</code></td><td>what shows when the flag is off: nothing, unless given</td></tr>
						<tr><td>children</td><td>what shows when the flag is on</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>{"<FlagsContext value={…}>"}</code> is a <a href={"${Paths.components}#contexts"}>context</a>
				that provides the flags to every <code>{"<Flagged>"}</code> under it (its file needs
				<code>import pf.Context</code>). Without one, every flag is off. <code>Flags.enabled(values, name)</code>
				reads one flag, where an <code>if</code> reads better.
			</p>

			<Heading id="guards">Other guards</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Guard</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.security_headers(Security.defaults)</code></td><td>adds the headers below to every answer, but not those it sets itself</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.body_limit(bytes)</code></td><td>refuses a larger body, <code>413</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.methods([\"GET\", \"POST\"])"}</code></td><td>refuses any other method, <code>405</code>, with <code>Allow</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.hosts([\"example.com\", \"*.example.com\"])"}</code></td><td>refuses any other <code>Host</code>, <code>400</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.ips({ allow, deny, trust })"}</code></td><td>refuses an address in <code>deny</code>, or not in <code>allow</code> when it lists any, <code>403</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.maintenance({ flag, message, retry_after_s, except })"}</code></td><td>while the flag is on, answers <code>503</code> with <code>Retry-After</code> and a small page, except under the paths in <code>except</code> (<code>/admin</code>, so you can turn it off again)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Guard.feature(flag, who)</code></td><td>answers <code>404</code> to anyone who doesn't have the flag on</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Guard.under(\"/api\", guard)"}</code></td><td>runs <code>guard</code> for paths under <code>/api</code> only</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Security.defaults</code> adds these headers to every answer, as <code>Security.headers</code>
				wrote them when this page was built:
			</p>
			<Code title="Security.headers(Security.defaults, { dev: Bool.False })" source={GuardHeaders.text} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The Content-Security-Policy fits a Roc Solid app: scripts and WebAssembly from the site's own
				origin (a page needs <code>'wasm-unsafe-eval'</code> to start its wasm under a CSP), inline styles
				(components write <code>{"style=\"…\""}</code>), and calls to the site's own server.
				<code>Security.directive</code> changes one directive (to allow images from another host, say).
				Under <code>--dev</code> there is no <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code>, and the CSP only
				reports: a violation shows in the console instead of breaking the page. The dev server's reload
				script is the site's own file (<code>/__reload.js</code>), so it is no violation.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				The logic is plain Roc, on no platform, so the server, the browser and a test decide alike.
				<code>roc test packages/guard/main.roc</code> runs its <code>expect</code>s.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>RateLimit</code></td><td>a limit: <code>per_second</code>, <code>per_minute</code>, <code>per_hour</code>, <code>per(n, window_ms)</code>, <code>.sliding()</code>; <code>fresh</code>, <code>check(state, now)</code>, <code>idle</code>, <code>headers</code>, <code>explain</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Tally</code></td><td>many clients' states by key, bounded: <code>Tally.hit(tally, limit, key, now, room)</code>; <code>to_str</code>, <code>from_str</code>, <code>shard</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>ClientIp</code></td><td><code>ClientIp.of(trust, peer, headers)</code>, by <code>Direct</code>, <code>Header(name)</code>, <code>Hops(n)</code> or <code>Proxies(cidrs)</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Ip</code></td><td>IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and ranges: <code>parse</code>, <code>to_str</code>, <code>cidr</code>, <code>cidrs</code>, <code>within</code>, <code>within_any</code>, <code>key</code>, <code>private</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Flag</code></td><td>a flag in code: <code>Flag.define(name, about, rule)</code>, <code>.in_env(env, rule)</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Flags</code></td><td>rules (<code>on</code>, <code>off</code>, <code>rollout(percent)</code>, <code>only(users, groups)</code>), <code>is_on</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>enabled</code>, <code>set</code> and <code>reset</code> for overrides, <code>describe</code> for an admin page</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Security</code></td><td>security headers: <code>defaults</code>, <code>directives</code>, <code>directive</code>, <code>csp</code>, <code>headers</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Hash</code></td><td>a small, stable hash of text, the same on every machine: <code>of</code>, and <code>bucket</code>, which puts a user in or out of a rollout and a client in a tally</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Checks</code></td><td>checks on a request: <code>methods</code>, <code>hosts</code>, <code>body_size</code>, <code>ips</code>, <code>maintenance</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The server half: <code>Guard</code> holds the guards, <code>FlagStore</code> the flags while the
				server runs, and <code>Kept</code> the rate limits' tallies. Only the server compiles these three.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.guard_middleware_api.file} source={Sources.guard_middleware_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.guard_flagstore_api.file} source={Sources.guard_flagstore_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The <a href="https://github.com/Roc-Solid/rocx/blob/master/packages/guard/README.md">package's
				README</a> has the whole API in one place.
			</p>

			<Heading id="status">Tested, and limits</Heading>
			<p>
				Every module is tested by its <code>expect</code>s, and the middleware in
				<code>packages/guard/example/</code>, asked over HTTP. Its login limit answers <code>429</code> on
				the sixth try with the headers above, also to clients whose <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> isn't an
				address (they share one count). Its admin endpoint answers <code>403</code> to anyone but an admin.
				Flag overrides turn its feature route and maintenance mode on and off while it runs, and outlive a
				restart in its SQLite file.

			</p>
			<Note>
				The server reads a request's body, up to 16 MiB (or its <code>--max-body</code>), before any
				middleware runs, so <code>Guard.body_limit</code> refuses a large body after it has arrived, not
				before.
			</Note>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	.guard-checkouts {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, max(14rem, calc((100% - var(--space-3)) / 2))), 1fr));
		gap: var(--space-3);
		margin: var(--space-4) 0;
	}

	.guard-checkouts p {
		margin: 0;
		padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--bg-raised);
	}
</style>
